1.5 Synchronization Conflict Management

Implement proper conflict detection.

Example:

If an administrator edits a saving record online while another authorized officer edits the same record offline, the system must not silently overwrite either version.

Provide:

Record Version.


Last Modified Time.


Modified By.


Server Version.


Local Version.


Conflict Details.


Conflict Resolution Screen.


Possible resolution actions:

Keep Server Version.


Review Local Version.


Merge permitted non-financial fields.


Submit Correction Request.


Authorized Manual Resolution.


Financial transactions must never be automatically merged in a way that duplicates or changes monetary values.

Use transaction IDs and idempotency keys.

Never silently delete or overwrite financial records during synchronization.

Use reversal and correction entries for financial adjustments.

1.6 Offline Security

Offline access must follow strict security rules.

Cache only authorized information.


Never cache another member's private financial information for a member account.


Never store plain-text passwords or PINs.


Never store service-account credentials locally.


Encrypt sensitive locally stored data where practical using secure device-bound key management.


Clear sensitive cached data when a user logs out where appropriate.


Provide session expiration.


Require reauthentication after session expiry.


Do not allow expired offline permissions to grant new privileges.


Do not permit offline permission escalation.


Do not allow an offline user to access System settings unless an authorized, previously established offline policy permits it.


Clearly indicate when information may be outdated.


Offline access must use a previously authenticated, securely provisioned session with an appropriate expiry policy.

Do not pretend that an offline login can validate credentials against Google Sheets.

For first-time login, password reset, account recovery, or expired sessions, require an internet connection.

